\f1\fs22 No one was as qualified for the task as John Stoat. Everyone who looked back at him from his bathroom mirror agreed. \'91No one else has the talent,\'92 every one of them told him. They also told him he wasn\'92t as bald as colleagues and holders of restraining orders claimed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There isn\'92t anyone smarter than me,\'92 Johnny said to the reflection in the mirror, then fastidiously brushed back the hair he didn\'92t have. His mirror-friend bared their perfect Airborne teeth for him to inspect. Johnny nodded with inflated approval.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the living room, Leviathan\'97Johnny\'92s black-and-tan cavadoodle\'97waited by the balcony windows, his eyes wide with expectant desperation. \'91Oh, does poor Levi need to go doo-doos?\'92 Johnny crooned. Levi made his best impression of crossing his legs, and whimpered. All too sharp in Levi\'92s doggy mind was the memory of his master\'92s retribution the last time he\'92d failed to wait. Poor Levi couldn\'92t understand the fuss; he was certain he\'92d improved the scent of the snow leopard rug on Johnny\'92s cigar room floor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who\'92s a good boy for waiting?\'92 Johnny praised as he hoiked Levi under one arm and slid open the glass door. \'91You\'92re a good boy, yes you are.\'92 Levi licked Johnny\'92s face until Johnny loaded him into the litter harness. Levi waited patiently yet nervously while Johnny wound the ratchet on the boom, lifting Levi into the air. Johnny stared into the glare of the morning sun, checking for elephant-sized shadows looming above. Seeing none, he resolved to get this business done quickly, swung Levi over the balcony railing and steadied the harness chain. Levi stopped panting, his face a picture of relief as, dangling in mid air dozens and dozens of storeys above the ground,  he: 1) cocked his leg, and 2) lifted his tail and grunted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the less than thirty seconds it took Levi to find complete relief, Johnny smiled with wicked pleasure. Normally, the Layer would dissolve any pet litter before it passed through and splattered the ground. But this day had ascended bright and sunny; an infrequent westerly conquered the prevailing \cf0 southeast trade winds,\cf3  \cf2 and blew much of the Layer out over the Tasman Sea. Johnny watched as shards of Levi\'92s stream and bombing raid descended. He studied the movements of ant-sized pedestrians, and grinned with satisfaction as they scattered. He imagined he saw at least one brushing excrement out of hair and off clothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Cloudborne had learned to hate sunny days, and the Airborne loved that they hated them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny\'92s hubris exisccated as a barrage of thick, mucousy, cellulose-rich clumps, the size of Levi\'92s head, fell from the sky. One struck the harness chain and broke apart. Levi yelped. So did Johnny, who scrambled to retrieve Levi and get him under the cover of the balcony above.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax you, you taxing psychopath!\'92 Johnny shouted at the sky, and soberly noted the sound of his voice would not reach the penthouse rooftop. 
\f2\i How bad is my luck?
\f1\i0  he complained to himself. As he gathered quivering, traumatised, face-licking Levi in his arms, he cursed again the day he bought the apartment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He should\'92ve have researched it more, but he couldn\'92t see past the prestige of living in the same apartment as the flying electric pimp-car tycoon, Pretorius Voltworthy. He couldn\'92t resist seizing the bargain. The fools selling the place clearly had no understanding of its value: with its height above the Layer and its north easterly aspect; and with panoramic views of the Layer over the harbour sweeping around to views of the Layer over the heads, and even as far as the Layer as it obscured the view of the ocean. Johnny looked out from the balcony on inspection day, and knew his gaze beamed over the crushed hopes and dreams of so many Cloudborne (well, they deserved it, didn\'92t they?), and it filled him with a sense of pride and belonging.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He belonged to this part of the city. He deserved it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny couldn\'92t resist pulling out his tablet and flicking the deposit to the agent. He couldn\'92t exploit the sucker vendors fast enough. Maybe even Sarah would see how astute he was now. He imagined telling her about it over an expensive degustation with matching expensive wines served by matching apple-polishing waiters. Perhaps she\'92d even acquiesce to coming back after dinner, and she\'92d be so grateful, she\'92d \'85 well, she would, wouldn\'92t she? Finally?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny stewed himself so deeply in reverie and longing for Sarah, he couldn\'92t hear the quiet, rational voice in his head urging caution. If he\'92d heeded that voice, he may have researched proposed developments for the building, and would have discovered that approval had been granted Pretorius Voltworthy to keep six pet elephants on the balconies of his penthouse. He\'92d also gained approval to construct six industrial-strength gantries for swinging elephants in harnesses out over balcony railings for the same purpose as Johnny afforded his beloved Levi. If Johnny had preformed the merest diligence, he would have discovered one of those elephant-sized pet-litter harnesses was to be constructed directly above his hastily bought apartment. Which was why it was so taxing cheap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Husk was said to be infatuated with elephants (which Johnny scoffed at because the word shared its suffix with 
\f2\i sycophant
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i untriumphant
\f1\i0 ; Husk would do well to dissociate from words like that
\f2\i )
\f1\i0 , but only the African variety which, Husk thought were majestic, and so reflected his own presence in the world, whereas their Indian cousins looked, according to Husk, \'91like something Rick Moranis created in 
\f2\i Honey I Blew Up the Mole Rat\'92
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course, the thought of keeping pet elephants may seem excessive to some smug inhabitants of other universes, even impossible. But such incredulousness would be ignoring a rich man\'92s need to gas his ever-inflating ego, and the resources he has at his disposal for the most preposterous of excesses. Let\'92s face it, the cost of hiring zoologists, habitat designers, dieticians, wranglers, elephant psychologists and even the cost of upgrading the building\'92s goods lifts are piffling trifles in the ledgers of a billionaire\'92s tax minimisation accounts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Besides, Goff Bustus had giraffes in his penthouse (a metaphor for his sanity as well as a physical reality), and Husk couldn\'92t stand the thought of being bested (or busted) by anyone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91An elephant only has to shit twelve to fifteen times a day,\'92 Johnny lamented. \'91How does it always seem to correspond when you need too, Levi?\'92 He snuggled his cavadoodle and set him down on a lounge. \'91Daddy has to go to work now.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As Johny descended in the lift to the Rail floor (Shakti Enterprises had a private carriage on all rail services in the city), he ruminated over Levi\'92s close call, and ground his sense of impotence between his teeth. Try as he might to focus on this latest insult, he failed to ward off memories of an even greater injustice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Levi was the second dog Johnny had owned since occupying the apartment. The first, a cavadoodle named Gigi (short for Gigantor), had suffered worse than being pelted with elephant droppings. She had been pelted with an entire elephant.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Rockets can explode, self-driving cars can collide with parked vehicles, and pet litter gantries for elephants can suffer metal fatigue from the weight of\'97who\'92d\'92ve thought?\'97elephants. They can collapse with screeching anger and fall. And collect any pooping cavadoodle on the way down. RIP poor Gigi. She yelped but once.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Exhibiting the best side of his MLPD (Multiple Lack of Personality Disorder), Pretorius Voltworthy didn\'92t give a tax about the elephant. It was one of three given him by the African Pirate Collective as a sign of gratitude for Leon peppering the Congo Basin with rockets tipped with superseded pimp cars. Why weren\'92t they tipped with warheads? Apparently there\'92s nothing more terrifying in a jungle than a battery fire. It was a short war.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>First responders to the scene of the elephant quickly hypothesised that, given the trauma inflicted on the regal beast, the terminal velocity of African and Indian elephants could not, for all empirical and epistemological reasons, be distinguished. However, the accident did settle another point of conjecture popularised across social media: that the Layer, no matter how caustic, cannot dissolve the carcass of an elephant (African not Indian).\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny enjoyed a typically smooth trip to work, despite his upset over the near death of his beloved\'97or at least, transactionally appreciated\'97pet. Once ensconced in the Rail\'92s Shakti Enterprises private carriage, he could relax while similar carriages from all over the city were marshalled in a cascading process, in which they were separated from public trains and joined with other Shakti carriages until whole eight-carriage Shakti trains converged on Shakti Central, an office compound in the inner west built on the ruins of a greyhound track and a biscuit factory. This was not Johnny\'92s final destination. From the train, Johnny took a Shakti Hover-copt over the Once-Blue Mountains to his facility, which included labs, a control room, and a pair of bunkers in a large excavation originally dug thirty metres down for a subway station and later earmarked as a bomb shelter for a nuclear conflict that never came.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the control room, Johnny watched a series of monitors, many of which provided views into the containment bunker where Orion Contolan\'97a doctor in comparative mythical physics and a chief technician on Johnny\'92s Gyges Project\'97gingerly yet calmly placed the disc on a broad white polymer bench under an ominous and expensive radiation emitter that clung from the middle of the bunker roof like a monstrous metallic bat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny had been watching for shimmers on the surface of the disc, but detected none. He guessed it was safe to proceed. \'91Five-second bursts at 500 GeV, commencing in thirty seconds,\'92 Johnny announced coldly into his headset mic. \'91Clear the room unless you want to cook from the inside out.\'92 Johnny had already sealed the lab tight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Orion stabbed at the exit keypad as the other techs crowded him from behind. \'91Wait a minute, sir!\'92 he called, his professional demeanour beginning to drown in a rising tide of panic. \'91The door is locked.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> \'91Hurry, little man,\'92 Johnny answered as he rocked back in his chair and tented his fingers. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s taxing stuck!\'92 Orion cried.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Stop the test,\'92 shouted another tech.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Stop!
\f1\i0 \'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'913\'85  2\'85\'92 Johnny flicked a switch on his console which piped full-volume sound into the bunker of electricity arcing. The technicians all fell to the floor, buried their heads between their knees and threw their arms around their shoulders.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a few seconds, Johnny flicked the thunder show off. In the silence that followed, one of the technicians tipped over on his side and clasped at his chest. Others began to whimper. Johnny laughed. \'91False alarm.\'92 He released the exit lock; the techs heard a distinct, life-saving click.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tax you!\'92 a tech screamed. \'91I think Saunders has had a heart attack.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The techs huddled around Saunders, caught between concern for their colleague and primal need to flee danger. The greater share succumbed to their terror.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny sullenly ordered medics to the test room with a stretcher.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Later, after swapping in a new shift of technicians, and the test was reset, Johnny considered running the prank all over again, but some microgram of responsibility that had lodged deeply and secretly in Johnny\'92s personality rose up. After he\'92d allowed the techs to leave the test room and take up their stations at their posts, Johnny sighed and proceeded with the test.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The emitter escalated from a hum to a high-pitched squeal as it bombarded the disc with gamma rays. After twenty cycles the emitter shut down, and technicians engaged instruments from around the periphery to roll in on their pneumatic wheels, and deploy their sensors on articulators around the disc. They pushed spectacles back on their noses, scratched their necks, and adjusted their underwear as they studied their monitors. \'91No measurable phenomena,\'92 one announced over team comms. \'91No change,\'92 from another. \'91Stasis maintained throughout the test window,\'92 another reported.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing?\'92 Johnny checked, as the reptilian parts of his brain shut down any consideration that the project could be a total failure. \'91Gamma radiation has no effect whatsoever?\'92 
\f2\i Don\'92t think about it, magic neo cortex
\f1\i0 . \'91What else can we try?\'92 asked himself, forgetting  he was murmuring into his mic.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir \'85\'92 one brave soul started, perhaps not realising the next thing he said could be his last as a Shakti Research employee. \'91We have tried chemicals, electric current, magnetism, heat, cold, all manner of radiation, MRI, ultrasound, tactile imaging, positron emission tomography, x-rays and neutrino showers. We have even tried bashing it with a hammer.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny recalled his conversation with Sarah at that abominable Elvi bar the night it was raided by PAPER agents, the night Sarah showed him the Gyges disc, then held it back from him. 
\f2\i Well, I\'92ve got the taxing thing now, and no one is going to stop me from unlocking its secrets.
\f1\i0  \'91You haven\'92t tried spinning it, have you?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Technicians furtively eyed each other, and knew for a certainty they were thinking alike. 
